{
  "_comment": "Worker wake files (BSUP-5): `workers/<id>/control` (the owner's tap: park, resume, restart, fresh; same JSON as a wake) and `workers/<id>/wake` (a message pointer; an older bridge also writes a tap there, and so does a newer one while the supervisor has not said it reads `control`, workers.json supervisor.reads_control). One tick of a reader takes `control` FIRST, then `wake`, each by rename, and carries out what it took in that order. Each vector: `writes`, applied in order to the thread's folder before the tick (a later write to the same file replaces the earlier one; a string `content` is written verbatim, an object as JSON). The tick is for thread `trip` (the starting state: `session`), with the reader's clock at `now` and no earlier capped wakes. `acts`: the ordered actions the reader carries out. `audits`: the reader's audit lines for what it dropped or refused, in order (event and why; notices are not listed). After the tick neither `control` nor `wake` exists in the folder, whatever happened to each. Read by apps/bridge/test/supervisor/wake-reader.test.ts and by the Mac supervisor's Swift twin. Hand-written: change a case only with the protocol.",
